TITLE = "Roof Rail Types Explained: Bare, Raised, Flush, Fixed Points and Tracks"
META = ("Bare roof, raised rails, flush rails, fixed points, tracks or a removable top: how to tell which roof you have, "
        "which foot and fit kit it needs and what it carries.")

FAQ = [
 ("How do I tell what type of roof rails I have?",
  "Stand beside the car and look along the roof edge. A rail held up on posts with daylight under it is a raised rail; Yakima's test is whether you can slide your hand underneath. A rail sealed to the roof along its whole length is a flush rail. A smooth roof with nothing on it is a bare roof, which makers also call a naked roof. Four small covers or flaps in the roof trim mean fixed mounting points. A rail with a groove down the center is a track, and a lip along the roof edge is a rain gutter. If bars already cross the roof, you have factory crossbars on top of one of these."),
 ("What is the difference between raised rails and flush rails?",
  "The gap. A raised rail stands off the roof on end posts, so a foot can wrap or clamp around it, and it usually needs no vehicle-specific kit. A flush rail is attached along its length with no opening underneath, so the foot has to grip the sides of the rail and needs a kit shaped for that car. The two are not interchangeable: Thule sells separate Evo Raised Rail and Evo Flush Rail feet, and on the 2023 Kia Telluride the site's guide found the same Thule bar system at $544.90 for raised rails and $704.85 for flush rails on etrailer."),
 ("Can I put a roof rack on a car with a bare roof?",
  "Yes. A bare roof takes a clamp-style system whose feet sit on the roof edge and hook into the door jambs with vehicle-specific clips. Thule's foot for this is the Evo Clamp, described on its site as feet for cars without roof attachment points, and Yakima's is the BaseLine with BaseClips. It is the most expensive way to add bars: etrailer listed Yakima BaseLine systems at $604.75 to $773.75 for vehicles in the site's guides. The kit also sets where the bars sit, so check your cargo box's spread range against it before you buy the box."),
 ("What is a fit kit and do I need one?",
  "A fit kit is the vehicle-specific part of a rack: the pads, clips or brackets that shape a universal foot to one roof. Bare roofs, flush rails and fixed points need one. Raised rails usually don't, which is why raised-rail racks cost less. Examples from the site's guides: Thule kit 6008 for the 2020–2025 Hyundai Palisade's flush rails, kit 6095 for the Kia Telluride's, and a Fixpoint kit for the Toyota Highlander. Yakima's catalogue lists vehicle-specific BaseClips, SightClips and Landing Pads for the same job. The kit number comes from the maker's fit guide for your year and trim, not from the foot."),
 ("What is the difference between dynamic and static roof load?",
  "Dynamic load is what the roof can carry while the car is moving. Static load is what it can hold when parked, which is the number rooftop-tent buyers look for. The gap is large. Tesla rates its Model 3 rack at 150 lb dynamic and 495 lb static. The 2019–2024 Subaru Forester's standard rails are rated at 176 lb while driving and 700 lb parked. Everything you carry on the road, including the tent itself, has to fit inside the dynamic figure. The static figure only covers the extra weight of people once you have stopped."),
 ("Does the roof weight limit include the rack itself?",
  "Yes. The dynamic limit covers everything above the roof: feet, crossbars, the box or carrier, and the cargo. Crossbar sets in the site's guides weigh from about 12 lb to 25 lb, and platforms far more. On the Ford Bronco, where owners cite a 110 lb dynamic limit from Ford's documentation, a 67 lb platform leaves roughly 43 lb for gear. A bar's own rating doesn't raise the limit either. Brand-name systems are usually rated at 165 lb and some budget bars print 300 lb or more, but the lowest of the roof limit and the rack rating is what applies."),
 ("What is crossbar spread and why does it matter for a cargo box?",
  "Spread is the distance between the front and rear bar. Every cargo box, bike tray and tent lists a minimum and maximum spread its hardware can reach. On raised rails and most flush rails you slide the bars to suit. On fixed points and bare-roof kits the car sets it: about 28 in on the Tesla Model 3, about 35.5 in on the Model Y's factory rack, and about 24.5 in for a Rhino-Rack Vortex on the 2023 Jeep Grand Cherokee, according to etrailer. A box that needs 32 in will not mount on a 24.5 in spread, however good the bars are."),
 ("Why does my trim have a different roof from other versions of the same model?",
  "Because makers use the roof to separate trims. Base trims often ship bare while higher trims get rails, and off-road trims get their own hardware. In the site's data the 2019–2025 Toyota RAV4 LE has a bare roof and the XLE and up have raised rails, with a different rail again on the Adventure and TRD Off-Road. The Kia Telluride's X-Line and X-Pro have raised rails while other trims are flush. Subaru's Wilderness models use a ladder-type rail. Crossbar listings name trims for this reason, so read the exclusions and look at your own roof before ordering."),
 ("Can I use factory crossbars with aftermarket accessories?",
  "Usually, if the accessory's clamp fits the bar's shape and size. Check the accessory's bar compatibility list, because thick factory bars can need a different mounting kit. Factory bars also have limits of their own. Autoblog notes the Subaru Outback's swing-in bars are narrower than aftermarket bars, which makes two bikes or a box plus a kayak hard to fit. Subaru says its genuine Forester aero bars do not support rooftop tents. Tesla's bars have a T-slot, so T-bolt carriers slide in. The sockets or mounts usually fix the spread, so measure it before buying a box."),
 ("Can I put a roof rack on a Jeep Wrangler or Ford Bronco?",
  "Yes, but not a standard rail kit. Both have removable tops, so the rack has to match the top. The site's data describes Wrangler racks as bolting to the hardtop or using a cage-style rack. On the 2021–2026 Bronco, crossbar kits and platforms attach to the hardtop's mounting points or rain gutters, and soft tops need a bridging rack. Door count matters, because a 4-door platform won't fit a 2-door hardtop. The Bronco's roof limit is also small, with owners citing 110 lb while driving, so a light crossbar kit or half rack often makes more sense than a heavy platform."),
]

